# --- s025 Book I d.3 q.10 = Ord. I d.3 p.3 q.3 (Vat. nn.554-568)
n("n456","s025-01","Decima questio tertie distinctionis","la","source","= Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 3 p. 3 q. 3 (Vat. nn. 554–568): *utrum principalior causa notitiae genitae sit obiectum in se vel in specie praesens, vel ipsa pars intellectiva animae*. A *principalis cause* is a syntactic slip (nominative needed) shared with B.","certain")
n("n457","s025-01","mouet non motum","la","source","*Ord.* n. 554 (the first principal argument). Cf. Arist. *De an.* III 10, 433b11–18 (the object of appetite moves without being moved) and *Metaph.* XII 7, 1072a26.","certain")
n("n458","s025-01","patet de sole et homine. 2°. phisicorum","la","source","Arist. *Phys.* II 2, 194b13. Gorris's *ad oppositum* (the universal cause concurs principally) is not Scotus's (*Ord.* nn. 557–558: the more actual form is more active; *ens secundum quid*). Instead it anticipates Scotus's first reason in the reply (n. 559).","certain")
n("n459","s025-02","quando cause se habent sic quod quedam est illimitata","la","source","*Ord.* n. 559, paraphrased: *quando causarum ordinatarum altera est indeterminata ad multos effectus, et alia determinata… illa quae est illimitata videtur esse principalior et perfectior*. Gorris omits the second reason (n. 560: the intellect acting, the object co-acts, *est enim in potestate nostra intelligere*).","certain")
n("n460","s025-03","obiectum beatificum vt clare visum","la","source","*Ord.* n. 561, nearly verbatim (*posset poni habere totam causalitatem respectu visionis, aut principaliorem quam pars intellectiva, et hoc propter excellentiam talis obiecti et defectum partis intellectivae*).","certain")
n("n461","s025-04","lux creata vel intellectus agens quod pro eodem accipio","la","commentary","The identification of the Augustinian 'created light' with the Aristotelian agent intellect is not in *Ord.* p. 3 q. 3. Note the first person (*accipio*, *dico*): Gorris is speaking in his own voice here, or reporting a source that speaks in the first person. Cf. Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 3 n. 268 (illumination) and *Quodl.* q. 15.","to-verify")
n("n462","s025-04","duplicem potentiam. quarum vna est omnia facere. alia omnia fieri","la","source","Arist. *De an.* III 5, 430a14–15 (*est intellectus quidam quo omnia fieri, alius quo omnia facere*).","certain")
n("n463","s025-04","quatuor effectus essentialiter ordinatos","la","commentary","The fourfold co-causality of the agent intellect (species with the phantasm; act with the species; disposition to the habit; habit with the species) is a school formula. I have not found it as such in the *Ordinatio*. Cf. Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 3 nn. 359–364 (species caused by agent intellect and phantasm as partial causes), *Ord.* II d. 3 p. 2 q. 1, and *Quodl.* q. 15. It may come from a later Scotist (Meyronnes? Petrus Tartaretus?).","to-verify")
n("n464","s025-05","spiritus illam de cellula in cellulam deferentes","la","commentary","The doctrine of the inner senses (common sense, imagination, estimative, memory) located in the cerebral ventricles (*cellulae*), with the animal spirits as carriers, is Avicennian–Galenic medical psychology. Cf. Avicenna, *De anima* I 5 and IV 1, and Albert, *De homine*. It is unusual in a Scotus commentary and probably reflects Gorris's own teaching, or that of the Toulouse school.","certain")
n("n465","s025-05","mouetur non motus","la","textual","So A (and B). The sense requires *mouet non motus* ('moves without being moved'), answering the initial argument. *mouetur* is probably an error of the common exemplar. The translation follows the sense.","uncertain")
n("n466","s025-05","causa principalis in operando licet non in mouendo","la","source","Cf. *Ord.* n. 564: *in utraque actione obiectum est secundarium movens, licet non sit motum…*. Gorris simplifies Scotus's reply.","certain")

# --- B collation (full): B38L–B39L
n("n1103","s025-01","cause noticie genite","la","textual","B: *causa noticie in se genite*.","certain")
upd("n457", append=" B omits the minor *Sed obiectum mouet non motum* (homoeoteleuton on *motum*).")
n("n1104","s025-01","intellectus autem non mouet nisi motus","la","textual","B: *Intellectus non mouet respectu motus*, apparently a misreading of *nisi motus*; A gives the required sense.","probable")
n("n1105","s025-02","intellectionum modo nobis naturaliter conuenientium","la","textual","B: *intellectionis*, omitting *modo nobis naturaliter conuenientium*.","certain")
n("n1106","s025-02","naturaliter cognita virtutem","la","textual","B: *naturaliter cognita habent virtutem*, supplying the verb.","certain")
